[preflight] snapshot 14 files from assets/ReCodeAgent/data/tool_projects/crust/aes128-simd/c
[delegate] analyst <- Explore the source codebase and produce a source map and brief that cites every module, identifying entry points, dependencies, and key abstractions.
[phase] Contract
[delegate] architect <- Produce the migration contract at analysis/brief.md based on analysis/source-map.md: define the target-language (Rust) crate layout, map every C module/function to its Rust equivalent, specify how the SSE intrinsics (g_mult_sse, Columns_sse, g_mult_sse_byte) will be handled (port to portable Rust or use std::arch), resolve the three functions declared in aes.h but never defined (drop, stub, or implement), and state the public API surface of the resulting crate. Cite every module from the source map.
[delegate] validator <- Validate the migration contract at analysis/brief.md against analysis/source-map.md and the actual C source files. Verify: (1) every C module and public function from the source map is accounted for in the contract's module mapping, (2) the 15 gap decisions are internally consistent and cite the correct source locations, (3) the target crate layout (9 src modules + 1 integration test) covers all mapped functions, (4) the 8 frozen contracts (block size, key size, round count, padding, SBOX/RSBOX/RCON values, GF polynomial, public API surface) match the actual C source, (5) the test strategy (1 translate + 8 characterize) is feasible given the source test file. Report any gaps, inconsistencies, or missing mappings.
[phase] Planning
[delegate] planner <- Read analysis/brief.md (the validated migration contract) and analysis/source-map.md. Produce the migration plan at meta/plan.md and the task list at meta/tasks.json that (1) partition all 9 target src modules plus the 1 integration test into an ordered set of batches, (2) order the batches bottom-up by dependency so each batch compiles against only already-migrated modules (e.g. cipher_utils/tables first, then keys, then matrix, then aes core, then padding/base64, then the public API facade, then the roundtrip test), (3) assign each batch a single translator task with a precise instruction naming the exact C source files and Rust target files to produce, and (4) include a final integration-test batch that runs the full Rust test suite. Cite the contract's module mapping and frozen contracts so the batches are traceable to analysis/brief.md.
[phase] Pilot
[delegate] translator <- Migrate batch-1 (tables/consts) per meta/plan.md and analysis/brief.md. Read source/src/aes.h (lines 10-12 for the Nb/Nk/Nr macros; lines 14-55 for the sbox, rsbox, and Rcon constant arrays). Produce two Rust files: src/tables.rs containing `pub const SBOX: [u8; 256]`, `pub const RSBOX: [u8; 256]`, and `pub const RCON: [u8; 11]` with values copied exactly from the C arrays (FIPS-197), and src/consts.rs containing `pub const NB: usize = 4`, `pub const NK: usize = 4`, `pub const NR: usize = 10`. Apply the contract's gap decisions: drop the `__attribute__((aligned(16)))` on the arrays, drop the `static const` header-guard pattern (use plain `pub const` in a module), and do not emit any C header guard. Do not use std::arch. Verify the SBOX/RSBOX/RCON values match FIPS-197 exactly and that NB=4, NK=4, NR=10 (frozen contracts). Ensure the module compiles standalone (no dependencies on other target modules).
[delegate] translator <- Migrate batch-2 (gf) per meta/plan.md and analysis/brief.md. Read source/src/cipher_utils.c (lines 14-32 for g_mult; lines 48-54 for Sub; lines 62-69 for InvSub). Produce src/gf.rs containing `pub fn gf_mult(a: u8, b: u8) -> u8` (GF(2^8) multiplication with irreducible polynomial 0x11b), `pub fn sub_bytes(state: &mut [u8; 16])` (apply SBOX to each byte), and `pub fn inv_sub_bytes(state: &mut [u8; 16])` (apply RSBOX to each byte). Apply the contract's gap decisions: port the SSE intrinsics to portable Rust (plain u8 arithmetic, no std::arch), use `#[inline]` on all functions, replace C pointer arithmetic with safe Rust slice indexing (state[row * 4 + col]). Do not use std::arch. Verify gf_mult is correct (e.g. gf_mult(0x57, 0x02) == 0x13 per FIPS-197 Appendix A.3), and that sub_bytes/inv_sub_bytes are exact inverses over all 256 byte values. Ensure the module compiles against batch-1 (src/tables.rs for SBOX/RSBOX) and no other target modules.
[delegate] translator <- Migrate batch-3 (shift) per meta/plan.md and analysis/brief.md. Read source/src/aes.c (lines 12-24 for Shift; lines 24-34 for InvShift). Produce src/shift.rs containing `pub fn shift_rows(state: &mut [u8; 16])` and `pub fn inv_shift_rows(state: &mut [u8; 16])`, where state is the 4x4 AES matrix stored row-major (state[row * 4 + col]). Apply the contract's gap decisions: port the SSE intrinsics to portable Rust (plain array rotation, no std::arch), use `#[inline]` on both functions, and replace C pointer arithmetic with safe Rust indexing. shift_rows rotates row r left by r positions (row 0 unchanged, row 1 left by 1, row 2 left by 2, row 3 left by 3); inv_shift_rows rotates row r right by r positions. Verify shift_rows and inv_shift_rows are exact inverses over a 16-byte state, and that the module compiles against batch-1 (src/consts.rs for NB) and no other target modules.
[delegate] translator <- Migrate batch-4 (mix) per meta/plan.md and analysis/brief.md. Read source/src/matrix.c (lines 3-14 for Columns; lines 14-36 for InvColumns). Produce src/mix.rs containing `pub fn mix_columns(state: &mut [u8; 16])` and `pub fn inv_mix_columns(state: &mut [u8; 16])`, where state is the 4x4 AES matrix stored row-major (state[row * 4 + col]) and each column is processed using the already-migrated `crate::gf::gf_mult`. Apply the contract's gap decisions: port the SSE intrinsics to portable Rust (plain gf_mult calls, no std::arch), use `#[inline]` on both functions, and replace C pointer arithmetic with safe Rust indexing. mix_columns applies the FIPS-197 MixColumns matrix (2,3,1,1) per column; inv_mix_columns applies the inverse matrix (14,11,13,9) per column. Verify mix_columns and inv_mix_columns are exact inverses over a 16-byte state, and that the module compiles against batch-2 (src/gf.rs for gf_mult) and batch-1 (src/consts.rs for NB) and no other target modules.
[delegate] translator <- Migrate batch-5 (key) per meta/plan.md and analysis/brief.md. Read source/src/keys.c (lines 14-52 for Add and Expansion) and source/src/aes.h (line 55 for RCON, lines 10-12 for Nk/Nr). Produce src/key.rs containing `pub fn add_round_key(state: &mut [u8; 16], round_key: &[u8; 16])` (XOR each state byte with the corresponding round-key byte) and `pub fn expand_key(key: &[u8]) -> Vec<[u8; 16]>` (AES key expansion producing NR+1 round keys of 16 bytes each, using the already-migrated `crate::tables::SBOX` and `crate::tables::RCON`). Apply the contract's gap decisions: port the SSE intrinsics to portable Rust (plain u8 arithmetic, no std::arch), use `#[inline]` on internal helpers, replace C pointer arithmetic with safe Rust indexing, and keep the Nk>6 branch for AES-192/256 support per the contract. Verify add_round_key is its own inverse (applying it twice with the same key restores the state) and that expand_key produces the correct number of round keys (NR+1 = 11 for AES-128) with the first round key equal to the input key. Ensure the module compiles against batch-1 (src/tables.rs for SBOX/RCON) and no other target modules.
[delegate] translator <- Migrate batch-6 (cipher) per meta/plan.md and analysis/brief.md. Read source/src/cipher.c (the Cipher and InvCipher functions) and source/src/aes.h (lines 10-12 for Nb/Nk/Nr, and the function prototypes) for context. Produce src/cipher.rs containing `pub fn cipher(state: &mut [u8; 16], round_keys: &[[u8; 16]])` (AES-128 encryption: initial AddRoundKey, then NR-1 full rounds of SubBytes/ShiftRows/MixColumns/AddRoundKey, then a final round of SubBytes/ShiftRows/AddRoundKey) and `pub fn inv_cipher(state: &mut [u8; 16], round_keys: &[[u8; 16]])` (AES-128 decryption: initial AddRoundKey with the last round key, then NR-1 inverse rounds of InvShiftRows/InvSubBytes/AddRoundKey/InvMixColumns, then a final round of InvShiftRows/InvSubBytes/AddRoundKey). Compose the already-migrated modules: `crate::key::add_round_key`, `crate::gf::{sub_bytes, inv_sub_bytes}`, `crate::shift::{shift_rows, inv_shift_rows}`, `crate::mix::{mix_columns, inv_mix_columns}`, and `crate::consts::{NB, NK, NR}`. Apply the contract's gap decisions: port any SSE intrinsics to portable Rust (plain u8 arithmetic, no std::arch), use `#[inline]` on internal helpers, and replace C pointer arithmetic with safe Rust indexing. Verify that cipher and inv_cipher are exact inverses over a 16-byte state (encrypt then decrypt restores the original state) using a known key, and that the module compiles against batch-2 (gf), batch-3 (shift), batch-4 (mix), batch-5 (key), and batch-1 (consts) and no other target modules.
